{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 220","reason":"Write P(x)=a+bx+cx^2+dx^3. The condition becomes a+c+(9−b)+(9−d)=9. These are four nonnegative integers summing to 9, so their upper bounds of 9 hold automatically. There are C(12,3)=220 solutions, including those with zero leading coefficients.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complement-digits-and-separators","label":"Complement digits and use separators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complement-digits-and-separators":["math:stars-and-bars","math:bijective-counting","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","math:bijective-counting"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: N = 14","reason":"For a term a^i b^j c^k d^l, all four exponents are positive and i+j+k+l ≤ N. Introducing the remaining constant selections and subtracting 1 from each variable exponent gives five nonnegative integers summing to N-4. Their number is C(N,4). Since C(14,4) = 1001 and this count increases with N ≥ 4, N = 14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h236","cnm_031","cnm_032"],"id":"exponents-and-separators","label":"Count exponent patterns","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exponents-and-separators":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_146","cnm_033"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_146","cnm_033","cnm_h236","cnm_031","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 405","reason":"For sorted elements a₁<⋯<aₖ, set bᵢ=aᵢ−(i−1). Then k≤b₁<⋯<bₖ≤16−k, giving C(17−2k,k) subsets. Only k=1,…,5 are possible, and the total is 15+78+165+126+21=405.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"distribute-gap-slack","label":"Count nonnegative gap variables","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distribute-gap-slack":["math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (190 ways)","reason":"After reserving six apples, the remaining allocations satisfy u+v+w=18 with u,v,w ≥ 0. Stars and bars gives C(20,2)=190.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_8_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"lower-bound-separator-count","label":"Remove the minimum allocations and count separator positions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"lower-bound-separator-count":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 1/5","reason":"The five deficits d_i sum to 2, giving C(6,4)=15 numbers. Their alternating digit sum is 7+2(d_2+d_4), which is divisible by 11 only when d_2+d_4=2. There are three such deficit pairs, so the probability is 3/15=1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"digit-deficit-compositions","label":"Count digit deficits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-deficit-compositions":["math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 15","reason":"The number of nonnegative solutions to g+c+p=4 is C(6,2)=15. Equivalently, fixing g=0,1,2,3,4 gives 5+4+3+2+1=15 selections.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_10_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"place-two-separators","label":"Use stars and bars","methodDirectNodeIds":{"place-two-separators":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p035","cnm_h236"],"id":"separator-encoding","label":"Encode the counts with separators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"separator-encoding":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_p035","cnm_h236"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 28","reason":"The number of solutions to c+o+p=6 is C(8,2)=28. Equivalently, fixing c from 0 through 6 gives 7+6+5+4+3+2+1=28 assortments.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cookie-count-separators","label":"Count nonnegative triples with separators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cookie-count-separators":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h236","cnm_p035"],"id":"encode-with-two-separators","label":"Encode the assortment with two separators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"encode-with-two-separators":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_h236","cnm_p035"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 224","reason":"Choose exponents b and c independently from 0 through 14, giving 225 pairs. Each forces a = 28 - b - c. This lies between 0 and 27 except when b = c = 0, so the coefficient is 225 - 1 = 224.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bounded-exponent-compositions","label":"Count bounded exponent triples","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bounded-exponent-compositions":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 46","reason":"For a number 1htu, the condition is u = 1 + h + t ≤ 9. The allowed pairs (h,t) number 9 + 8 + ⋯ + 1 = 45; all give numbers greater than 1000. Among numbers from 2000 through 2012, only 2002 qualifies. The total is 46.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"digit-sum-slack","label":"Stars and bars","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-sum-slack":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 84","reason":"Subtracting 1 from each die leaves seven nonnegative integers summing to 3. The upper bounds are automatic, so the count is C(9,6) = 84. Equivalently, the extra-point patterns contribute 7 + 7 × 6 + C(7,3) = 84.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stars-and-bars","label":"Distribute three extra points","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stars-and-bars":["math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (112)","reason":"With k factors, choose the position of the factor containing 3 in k ways. After assigning one power of 2 to every other factor, stars and bars gives C(5,k-1) exponent distributions. Thus D(96)=Σ[k·C(5,k-1)], for k=1 through 6, which is 1+10+30+40+25+6=112.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"distribute-prime-exponents","label":"Distribute prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distribute-prime-exponents":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"distinguish-three-factor","label":"Count by the number of factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distinguish-three-factor":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_h232"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 21","reason":"Let a, b, c count the $20, $50, $100 bills. Then 2a+5b+10c=80, so b=2k and a=5(8-k-c). Positivity requires k,c>=1 and k+c<=7, giving 6+5+4+3+2+1=21 collections.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"positive-compositions","label":"Count positive compositions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-compositions":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hundred-dollar-bundles","label":"Bundle bills into hundreds","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hundred-dollar-bundles":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p159","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p159","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 1/26","reason":"The disjoint blocks 1 through 5 and 6 through 10 each require an omission, so N <= 11; omitting 5 and 10 attains 11. For two omitted positions i < j, avoiding five consecutive chosen integers requires i <= 5, j >= 9, and j-i <= 5. The only pairs are (4,9), (5,9), and (5,10). Thus the probability is 3/C(13,2) = 1/26.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-run-deficits","label":"Count deficits in chosen runs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-run-deficits":["math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 17 or 18","reason":"Let m = floor((n-1)/2). For x+y = s, there are s-1 pairs and z is fixed, so the count is 1+2+...+(m-1) = m(m-1)/2 = 28. Hence m = 8 and n is 17 or 18.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"positive-compositions","label":"Introduce a positive slack variable","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-compositions":["math:floor-ceiling","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 784","reason":"Write B = (b,b) and D = (d,kd), with b,d positive integers. The area is bd(k - 1), so b·d·(k - 1) = 10^6 = 2^6·5^6. For each prime, its exponent 6 can be distributed among three factors in C(8,2) = 28 ways. These independent choices give 28^2 = 784 distinct parallelograms.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"distribute-prime-exponents","label":"Distribute prime exponents among three factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distribute-prime-exponents":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 855","reason":"There are C(19,3) = 969 unordered triples. A noncyclic triple has exactly one label preceding both others, so there are 19·C(9,2) = 684 such triples. Each of the remaining 285 cyclic triples has three eligible ordered listings, giving 855.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modular-gap-compositions","label":"Count bounded cyclic gaps","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modular-gap-compositions":["math:modular-congruence","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 47/256","reason":"Let F_m count length-m binary strings with no adjacent heads. Then F_0=1, F_1=2, and F_m=F_(m-1)+F_(m-2), giving F_7=34 and F_5=13. If a chosen person flips tails, there are 34 valid assignments; if heads, both neighbors must flip tails, leaving 13. Therefore the probability is (34+13)/256=47/256.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circular-gap-counting","label":"Count standing sets by circular gaps","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circular-gap-counting":["math:stars-and-bars","math:double-counting","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","math:double-counting","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 112","reason":"For k factors, choose the position containing 3 in k ways. Its exponent of 2 is nonnegative, while the other k-1 exponents are positive, giving C(5,k-1) assignments. Summing k·C(5,k-1) for k=1 through 6 gives 1+10+30+40+25+6=112.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231"],"id":"distinguish-three-factor","label":"Distinguish the factor containing 3","methodDirectNodeIds":{"distinguish-three-factor":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h231","cnm_h237","cnm_h232"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2/3","reason":"There are 29 possible consecutive pairs, each contained in C(28,3) subsets. The average is 29*C(28,3)/C(30,5)=29*(5*4)/(30*29)=2/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"consecutive-run-distribution","label":"Count consecutive runs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-run-distribution":["math:bijective-counting","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h239","cnm_h252"]},"nodeIds":["math:bijective-counting","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h239","cnm_h252"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 11","reason":"For S=2,3,4,5,6, the respective coordinate lower bounds are 0,1,1,2,2. The numbers of triples are 6,1,3,0,1, respectively, totaling 11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"slack-parity-count","label":"Encode by slacks and count by parity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"slack-parity-count":["math:bijective-counting","cnm_p156","math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:bijective-counting","cnm_p156","math:stars-and-bars"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 3/14","reason":"Let e_i=9-d_i. Then Σe_i=2, giving C(8,6)=28 digit strings; every digit is at least 7, so all are seven-digit numbers. Put b=e_2+e_4+e_6. The alternating digit sum is 7+2b, which is divisible by 11 only when b=2. Distributing both deficit units among the three even positions gives C(4,2)=6 possibilities. Thus the conditional probability is 6/28=3/14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"digit-deficit-compositions","label":"Count deficit compositions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-deficit-compositions":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-alternating-sums-and-use-separators","label":"Separate odd and even positions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-alternating-sums-and-use-separators":["cnm_h244","math:modular-congruence","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h244","math:modular-congruence","math:stars-and-bars","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 10","reason":"The allocations satisfy x + y + z = 6 with all three positive. Choosing two separators among five gaps gives C(5,2) = 10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_8_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p035","cnm_h231","cnm_h236"],"id":"two-dividing-gaps","label":"Divide a row into three groups","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-dividing-gaps":["math:stars-and-bars"]},"nodeIds":["math:stars-and-bars","cnm_p035","cnm_h231","cnm_h236"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 47/256","reason":"Let F_n count valid patterns on a line of n seats. Then F_0 = 1, F_1 = 2, and F_n = F_(n-1) + F_(n-2), giving F_7 = 34 and F_5 = 13. If the first person sits there are F_7 patterns; if they stand, both neighbors must sit, leaving F_5 patterns. Thus the probability is (34+13)/256 = 47/256.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-by-standing-number","label":"Count by number standing","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-by-standing-number":["math:stars-and-bars","math:double-counting","c